            "text": "{\n  \"primary_meaning\": \"The fleeting nature of earthly time and afflictions when contrasted with eternity, as well as the suddenness of divine action and eschatological change.\",\n  \"primary_basis\": [\n    \"1 Corinthians 15:52\",\n    \"2 Corinthians 4:17\",\n    \"Psalm 30:5\"\n  ],\n  \"alternatives\": [\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The instantaneous, unexpected nature of Christ's return and the resurrection of the dead.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"1 Corinthians 15:52\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"dominant\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The extreme brevity of God's wrath or discipline compared to His enduring grace and eternal favor.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Psalm 30:5\",\n        \"Isaiah 54:7-8\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"common\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"A decisive, appointed point of divine opportunity, crisis, or judgment, often reflecting the Greek concept of kairos.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Luke 19:44\",\n        \"Esther 4:14\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"meaning\": \"The fragility and ultimate transience of human life, wicked prosperity, or worldly power.\",\n      \"basis\": [\n        \"Job 20:5\",\n        \"Psalm 73:19\",\n        \"Luke 4:5\"\n      ],\n      \"prominence\": \"minority\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"has_settled_consensus\": false,\n  \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  \"notes\": \"While 'moment' is generally not treated as a rigid typological symbol in biblical hermeneutics, it serves as a major temporal motif. It is primarily used to draw a sharp contrast between the brevity of the temporal world (including affliction and divine discipline) and the permanence of eternity, or to emphasize the suddenness of eschatological events.\"\n}",
